<Fragment slot="heading">The philosophy degree I didn’t know I <span style="font-style:italic">needed</span></Fragment>
<p style="margin:0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Philosophy is easy to overlook in technology, and for years I overlooked mine too. I studied it about a decade before I moved into tech, when product management wasn’t on my radar, and I filed it under things I’d done before my real career started. Then I went back to study interactive design, app and game design, and the course coordinator pointed out that two of us in the class had philosophy backgrounds. He said it would be highly valuable in the work ahead. I hadn’t thought of it that way before.</p>
<p style="margin:22px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">The reputation is understandable. Nobody hires a product manager for their views on Kant. What the degree leaves you with is a habit: finding the assumption a claim is resting on, and arguing the other side properly before you reject it. The AI labs seem to have noticed. <a href="https://dailynous.com/2025/05/28/philosophers-and-anthropics-claude/" class="src">Daily Nous</a> has written about Amanda Askell, a philosopher who leads character training for Anthropic’s Claude.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Where it shows up</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Most product arguments are quietly arguments about words. Two teams disagree about whether something is a priority, or whether a feature is ‘done’, and each means something different by it. Asking what we mean by that sounds pedantic and usually saves a week. Prioritising has the same shape. You’re rarely choosing between a good idea and a bad one. You’re choosing between two good ones, and the team you turn down had a real reason to ask. I try to put their case well enough that they’d recognise it as theirs before I explain why we’re going the other way. Daniel Dennett made that the first of his <a href="https://www.themarginalian.org/2014/03/28/daniel-dennett-rapoport-rules-criticism/" class="src">rules for criticising someone</a>: state their position so clearly they say ‘I wish I’d thought of putting it that way’. If I can’t, I haven’t understood the decision yet.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-family:Newsreader,serif;font-size:25px;line-height:1.25;max-width:52ch">"If I can’t make the case for the thing I’m saying no to, I’m not ready to say no."</div>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">Why AI raises the stakes</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Most conventional software did what someone had specified. AI systems act on judgement, and Anthropic’s <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/constitution" class="src">constitution for Claude</a> says it generally favours ‘cultivating good values and judgment over strict rules and decision procedures’. Somebody still has to decide what good judgement looks like. When should an assistant refuse, and whose interests win when the user and the business want different things? OpenAI’s <a href="https://model-spec.openai.com/2025-04-11.html" class="src">Model Spec</a> answers with a chain of command in which higher-authority instructions override lower ones. A model can’t decide that for you. Neither can a metric, because someone had to decide what it should reward, and <a href="https://openai.com/index/measuring-goodharts-law/" class="src">OpenAI’s own research on Goodhart’s law</a> shows that optimising a proxy can make the thing you actually wanted worse. I’d bet most teams file these under edge cases to tidy up later. I think they’re most of the design work, and they’re questions philosophy has been working on for a very long time.</p>
<div style="margin-top:34px;font-size:17px;font-weight:600">The nugget</div>
<p style="margin:9px 0 0;font-size:15.5px;line-height:1.7;color:var(--body)">Next time two people are arguing past each other, ask each of them what they mean by the word doing the most work in their argument, usually something like ‘safe’ or ‘done’. Ten minutes of that tends to beat another hour of debate. And if there’s a philosophy graduate on your team, ask them. They’ve had years of practice and may never have thought to mention it.</p>
